    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Discovery — WMI Burst Recon (root\\cimv2 + root\\SecurityCenter2)", "technique": "T1047 + T1082", "what_happened": "At 2027-07-14 09:12:03 CEST, Wazuh fired rule 92220 'WMI burst query recon (>5 WQL queries in <10s)' on WIN-CORP-184 (10.108.57.184). Sysmon ETW WMI-Activity captured a rapid-fire sequence of five Get-CimInstance calls within ~8 seconds: SELECT * FROM AntiVirusProduct (root\\SecurityCenter2), SELECT * FROM Win32_GroupUser WHERE GroupComponent LIKE '%Administrators%' (root\\cimv2), SELECT * FROM Win32_LogonSession (root\\cimv2), SELECT Name,Version,Vendor FROM Win32_Product (root\\cimv2), SELECT * FROM Win32_ComputerSystem (root\\cimv2). The querying process image is powershell.exe (SYSTEM context), parentImage is cmd.exe spawned by wmiprvse.exe. The burst pattern — >5 distinct WQL classes in <10 seconds from the same PID — is the key signal separating post-exploitation tooling (Cobalt Strike beacon / Mimikatz wrapper) from legitimate scheduled inventory (SCCM ccmexec.exe, which is paced and targets hardware classes only).", "chain_reason": "Rule 92220 (T1082 TP/high) fired on ETW WMI-Activity correlation: >5 distinct WQL queries within 10s from powershell.exe running as SYSTEM on WIN-CORP-184.", "first_seen": "2027-07-14T09:12:03+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Discovery — Win32_Product enumeration / MSI repair audit smoke", "technique": "T1047", "what_happened": "At 09:12:19 CEST, rule 92221 'Win32_Product query — MSI repair audit smoke' fired. Win32_Product triggers an MSI repair scan on every installed product, generating excessive disk I/O. Sysmon captured msiexec.exe launched as a child of wmiprvse.exe for multiple product GUIDs within seconds — the diagnostic 'audit smoke' that confirms Win32_Product was actually executed, not just observed in ETW. This is the inventory-of-installed-software phase of the Cobalt Strike host-profiling playbook.", "chain_reason": "Rule 92221 (T1047 TP/medium): msiexec.exe child of wmiprvse.exe within the same 10-second burst window. The parent chain wmiprvse.exe → msiexec.exe is the canonical Win32_Product MSI-repair side-effect.", "first_seen": "2027-07-14T09:12:19+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Discovery — WMI LogonSession + GroupUser enumeration (credential hunt)", "technique": "T1087", "what_happened": "At 09:12:27 CEST, rule 92222 'WMI LogonSession + GroupUser enum (credential hunt)' fired. Within the same burst sequence, the attacker explicitly queried Win32_LogonSession (active logon tokens — useful for pass-the-hash / token impersonation) and Win32_GroupUser WHERE GroupComponent LIKE '%Administrators%' (local admin group membership — target selection for lateral movement). These two WQL classes together constitute a credential-hunt pattern: identify who is currently logged on AND which accounts hold local admin rights on this host. Combined with Win32_ComputerSystem (OS + domain membership) and AntiVirusProduct (AV evasion target identification), the enumeration covers the full Cobalt Strike 'host profile' checklist.", "chain_reason": "Rule 92222 (T1087 TP/high): ETW WMI-Activity logged WQL classes Win32_LogonSession and Win32_GroupUser within the burst window. The co-occurrence of these two classes is not observed in any legitimate scheduled inventory.", "first_seen": "2027-07-14T09:12:27+0200"}
